# Code owners — who GitHub requests a review from, automatically.
#
# The last matching pattern wins, so the catch-all comes first and the narrower rules below
# it. The point of the narrow ones is not that these files are more precious than the rest:
# it is that a change to the build, the release or the deployment files is the class of
# change this repository has repeatedly shipped broken, precisely because it is invisible in
# a diff full of application code.
#
# This file only requests reviewers. It does not *require* them — that is a branch
# protection rule on `main` ("Require review from Code Owners"), configured in the repository
# settings, and it is what turns this file from a convention into a gate.

*                               @devdownin

# CI, release and the supply chain. A change here can publish an artifact, mint a token, or
# silently stop running a check.
/.github/                       @devdownin
/.github/workflows/             @devdownin

# The build definition and the pinned toolchain.
/pom.xml                        @devdownin
/mvnw                           @devdownin
/mvnw.cmd                       @devdownin
/.mvn/                          @devdownin
/src/main/webapp/package.json   @devdownin
/src/main/webapp/package-lock.json @devdownin

# Everything that decides how the application is deployed and what it exposes. The bundled
# stacks bind to the loopback interface on purpose — the app has no authentication.
/Dockerfile                     @devdownin
/Dockerfile.release             @devdownin
/docker-compose*.yml            @devdownin
/.dockerignore                  @devdownin
/.env.example                   @devdownin

# Licence, security policy and governance.
/LICENSE                        @devdownin
/SECURITY.md                    @devdownin
/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md             @devdownin
/CONTRIBUTING.md                @devdownin
